--- a/docs/exception-handling.rst
+++ b/docs/exception-handling.rst
@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@
 Exception Handling
 ##################
 
-Schedule doesn't catch exceptions that happen during job execution. Therefore any exceptions thrown during job execution will bubble up and interrupt schedule's run_xyz function.
+Schedule doesn't swallow exceptions that happen during job execution. If a job raises, ``run_pending()`` and ``run_all()`` still run the remaining jobs, and then re-raise the first exception. A job that raised is rescheduled as usual, so it is not retried until its next scheduled run. If several jobs raise, only the first exception is re-raised; the others are logged.
 
 If you want to guard against exceptions you can wrap your job function
 in a decorator like this:
